An OSN is represented as a graph $G = (V, E)$; optional node features $X = \{x_v : v \in V\}$, and a set of labelled nodes partitioned into real accounts $R$ and fake accounts $F$ s.t. $R \cap F = \emptyset$.
The remaining nodes are unlabelled, i.e., $U = V \setminus (R \cup F)$. (In an unsupervised scenario, the labelled set is empty $R \cup F = \emptyset$.)

\noindent
\textbf{Output:} A classifier $f: V \to \{\text{real}, \text{fake}\}$
that assigns a label to each node in the graph.

\noindent
\textbf{Goal:} The primary objective is to maximise the correct identification of fake and real accounts. This is typically evaluated using performance metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score (see Table~\ref{tab:evaluationmetrics}).
