\section{Pullbacks of topological graph C*-algebras}

The aim of this section is to prove that under suitable conditions the topological graph C*-algebra of a regular adjunction graph is a pullback of the C*-algebras of the topological graphs being glued. To this end, we study the admissible pairs coming from kernels of $*$-homomorphisms induced by factor maps. Then we discuss how our result generalizes the pushout-to-pullback theorem proved in the context of directed graphs.

\subsection{Main result}

First, we identify the admissible pairs corresponding to kernels of $*$-homomorphisms induced by regular factor maps.
\begin{lemma}\label{factker}
Let $m:G\to E$ be a regular factor map and let $\mu:C^*(E)\to C^*(G)$ be the $*$-homo\-mor\-phism given by Proposition~\ref{functc*}. Then  $\ker\mu=I_\rho$ for the admissible pair $\rho=(m(G)^0,m(G)^0_{\rm sing})$, where $m(G)$ is the regular closed subgraph given by Proposition~\ref{facregsub}.
	\end{lemma}
	\begin{proof}
	Since $\mu$ is gauge-equivariant, $\ker\mu$ is a gauge-invariant ideal. Therefore, $\ker\mu$ has to be of the form $I_\rho$ for some admissible pair $\rho=(Y^0,Z^0)$ by Theorem~\ref{idealclass}. 
First, recall that
	\[
	t^0_E(C_0(E^0\setminus Y^0))=\ker\mu\cap t^0_E(C_0(E^0)).
	\]
For any $f\in C_0(E^0)$, $t^0_E(f)\in\ker\mu$ if and only if $f(m^0(v))=0$ for all $v\in G^0$ which implies that $Y^0=m^0(G^0)$. Consider the topological graph $E_\rho$ defined by~\eqref{katspush}, where $\rho=(m(G)^0,Z^0)$. There is a~regular factor map $n:E_\rho\to E$ given by the inclusions $m(G)\subseteq E$, $W_\rho^0\subseteq E$, and $r_{m(G)}^{-1}(W_\rho)\subseteq E^1$, which induces a~surjective $*$-homomorphism $\nu:C^*(E)\to C^*(E_\rho)$ whose kernel is $I_\rho=\ker\mu$ (see~\cite[Remark~3.14]{katsura3}). Next, one can prove that $m(G)$ is a regular closed subgraph of $E_\rho$ (see~\cite[Remark~3.24]{katsura2}). Hence, we obtain a regular factor map $k:G\to E_\rho$ inducing a $*$-homomorphism $\kappa:C^*(E_\rho)\to C^*(G)$. From the definitions of the maps involved, we get a~commutative diagram of $*$-homomorphisms:
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
	{C^*(E)} \arrow[rd,"\mu"] \arrow[d,"\nu"']& \\
    C^*(E_\rho) \arrow[r,"\kappa"] &  C^*(G).
\end{tikzcd}
\]
Indeed, it is clear that we have the equality $m=n\circ k$ of regular factor maps. Since $\ker\nu=\ker\mu$, we conclude that $\ker\kappa=\{0\}$. It follows from~\cite[Proposition~2.9]{katsura2} that $k$ is surjective which can only happen when $W_\rho=Z^0\cap m(G)^0_{\rm reg}=\emptyset$. In turn, we infer that $Z^0=m(G)^0_{\rm sing}$, which ends the proof. 
	\end{proof}

Now, let $E\cup_m F$ be a regular adjunction graph.  Then we have a commutative diagram
\begin{equation}\label{factdiag}
 \begin{tikzcd}[ampersand replacement=\&]
	\& {E\cup_mF} \\
	{E} \&\& {F} \\
	\& {G}
	\arrow[, right hook->, from=2-1, to=1-2]
	\arrow["m", <-, from=2-1, to=3-2]
	\arrow["p", <-, from=1-2, to=2-3]
	\arrow[, right hook->, from=3-2, to=2-3]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
inducing, by Proposition~\ref{functc*}, a commutative diagram of $\mathbb{T}$-equivariant $*$-homomorphisms
\begin{equation}\label{c*diag}
    \begin{tikzcd}[ampersand replacement=\&]
	\& {C^*(E\cup_mF)} \\
	{C^*(E)} \&\& {C^*(F).} \\
	\& {C^*(G)}
	\arrow["\pi_E"',->>, from=1-2, to=2-1]
	\arrow["\mu_E"', ->, from=2-1, to=3-2]
	\arrow["\pi_F", ->, from=1-2, to=2-3]
	\arrow["\mu_F", ->>, from=2-3, to=3-2]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}

Before proceeding further, we recall the notion of a pullback from category theory. Let $\mathcal{C}$ be any category and let $f_X:X\to Z$ and $f_Y:Y\to Z$ be morphisms in $\mathcal{C}$. A {\em pullback} of $f_X$ and $f_Y$ is an object $P$ in $\mathcal{C}$ together with morphisms $p_X:P\to X$ and $p_Y:P\to Y$ making the diagram
\begin{equation}\label{pulldiag}
\begin{tikzcd}[ampersand replacement=\&]
	\& P \\
	X \& \& Y \\
	\& Z
	\arrow["p_X"',->, from=1-2, to=2-1]
	\arrow["f_X"', ->, from=2-1, to=3-2]
	\arrow["p_Y", ->, from=1-2, to=2-3]
	\arrow["f_Y", ->, from=2-3, to=3-2]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
commute. Moreover, the triple $(P,p_X,p_Y)$ satisfies the following universal property: if there is $D\in\mathcal{C}$ and morphisms $g_X:D\to X$ and $g_Y:D\to Y$ such that $f_X\circ g_X=f_Y\circ g_Y$, then there is a~unique morphism $u:P\to D$ such that $g_X\circ u=p_X$ and $g_Y\circ u=p_Y$. It follows that $P$ is unique up to an isomorphism. We say that the diagram~\eqref{pulldiag} is a {\em pullback diagram} in the category $\mathcal{C}$. 

It is well known that in the category of C*-algebras and $*$-homomorphisms, the pullback of $\mu_1:A\to C$ and $\mu_2:B\to C$ is
\[
A\oplus_CB:=\{(a,b)\in A\oplus B~:~\mu_1(a)=\mu_2(b)\}
\]
together with the projections onto $A$ and $B$. If the $*$-homomorphisms $\mu_1$ and $\mu_2$ are $\mathbb{T}$-equivariant, then the same C*-algebra with the natural diagonal action and the same projections defines the pullback in the category of $\mathbb{T}$-C*-algebras and $\mathbb{T}$-equivariant $*$-homomorphisms.

The main theorem of the paper is:
\begin{theorem} \label{mainthm}
Let $E\cup_mF$ be a regular adjunction graph such that
\begin{equation}\label{maincond}
E^0_{\rm sing}\cap m(G)^0_{\rm reg}\subseteq p(F)^0_{\rm reg}.
\end{equation}
Then, the diagram~\eqref{c*diag} is a pullback diagram in the category of $\mathbb{T}$-C*-algebras and $\mathbb{T}$-$*$-ho\-mo\-mor\-phisms. In other words, there is a $\mathbb{T}$-equivariant $*$-isomorphism
\[
C^*(E\cup_m F)\cong C^*(E)\oplus_{C^*(G)} C^*(F).
\]
\end{theorem}

\noindent The following three lemmas are crucial for proving Theorem~\ref{mainthm}.
\begin{lemma}\label{capker}
Let $E\cup_mF$ be a regular adjunction graph. Then
\[
\ker\pi_E\cap\ker\pi_F=\{0\},
\]
where $\pi_E:C^*(E\cup_mF)\to C^*(E)$ and $\pi_F:C^*(E\cup_mF)\to C^*(F)$ are $*$-homomorphisms in the diagram~\eqref{c*diag}.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
First, note that $\ker\pi_E\cap\ker\pi_F$ is a~gauge-invariant ideal. Hence, $\ker\pi_E\cap\ker\pi_F=I_\rho$ for some admissible pair $\rho=(Y^0,Z^0)$ due to Theorem~\ref{idealclass}.
From Lemma~\ref{factker}, we conclude that 
$\ker\pi_E=I_{\rho_E}$ and $\ker\pi_F=I_{\rho_F}$ for the admissible pairs $\rho_E=(E^0,E^0_{\rm sing})$ and $\rho_F=(p(F)^0,p(F)^0_{\rm sing})$. Now, \cite[Proposition~2.13]{katsura3} implies that \[
(Y^0,Z^0)=\rho=\rho_E\cup\rho_F=(E^0\cup p(F)^0, E^0_{\rm sing}\cup p(F)^0_{\rm sing}).
\]
Combining~\cite[Proposition~2.10]{katsura3} and~\cite[Proposition~2.12]{katsura3}, we infer that
\[
(E\cup_mF)^0_{\rm sing}\subseteq E^0_{\rm sing}\cup p(F)^0_{\rm sing}\subseteq (E\cup_mF)^0\cap (E\cup_mF)^0_{\rm sing}=(E\cup_mF)^0_{\rm sing}.
\]
Hence, $\rho=((E\cup_mF)^0,(E\cup_mF)^0_{\rm sing})$. It follows that $\ker\pi_E\cap\ker\pi_F=\{0\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{preimage}
Let $E\cup_mF$ be a regular adjunction graph. Then
\[
\mu_F^{-1}(\mu_E(C^*(E)))\subseteq\pi_F(C^*(E\cup_mF)),
\]
where $\mu_E$, $\mu_F$, and $\pi_F$ are the $*$-homomorphisms in the diagram~\eqref{c*diag}.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
First, we prove that
\begin{equation}\label{t0incl}
\mu_F^{-1}(\mu_E(t^0_E(C_0(E^0))))\subseteq\pi_F(t^0_\cup(C_0((E\cup_mF)^0))).
\end{equation}
If $a\in\mu_F^{-1}(\mu_E(t^0_E(C_0(E^0))))$ then $\mu_F(a)=t_G^0(f\circ m^0)$ for some $f\in C_0(E^0)$, which can only happen if $a\in t^0_F(C_0(F^0))$. Since $\mu_F^0$ is surjective there exist $g\in C_0(F^0)$ such that $g|_{G^0}=f\circ m^0$.  By injectivity of $t_F^0$, we conclude that $a=t_F^0(g)$. Define a function $h:(E\cup_mF)^0\to\mathbb{C}$ by the formula
\[
h(v):=\begin{cases}f(v) & v\in E^0\\ g(v) & v\in F^0\setminus G^0\end{cases}.
\]
It is routine to check that $h$ belongs to $C_0((E\cup_mF)^0)$. Furthermore, $\pi_F(t^0_\cup(h))=t_F^0(h\circ p^0)=t_F^0(g)=a$ which proves~\eqref{t0incl}. Next, we prove that
\begin{equation}\label{t1incl}
\mu_F^{-1}(\mu_E(t^1_E(X_E)))\subseteq\pi_F(t^1_\cup(X_{E\cup_mF})).
\end{equation}
Let $a\in \mu_F^{-1}(\mu_E(t^1_E(X_E)))$. The proof is similar to the one above, namely by injectivity of $t^1_F$, we get that $a=t^1_F(\xi)$ for some $\xi\in X_F$ such that $\xi|_{G^1}=\eta\circ m^1$ for some $\eta\in X_E$. We define a function $\zeta:(E\cup_mF)^1\to\mathbb{C}$ by the formula
\[
\zeta(e):=\begin{cases}\eta(e) & e\in E^1\\ \xi(e) & e\in F^1\setminus G^1\end{cases}.
\]
Note that $\zeta$ is well defined and belongs to $C_b((E\cup_mF)^1)$. Furthermore, $\zeta\in X_{E\cup_mF}$. Indeed, using the definition of $r_\cup$, we infer that
\[
\langle\zeta,\zeta\rangle(v)=\begin{cases}\langle\eta,\eta\rangle(v) & v\in E^0\\\langle\xi,\xi\rangle(v) & v\in F^0\setminus G^0\end{cases}.
\]
Since $C^*(E)$ and $C^*(E\cup_mF)$ are C*-algebras generated by the images of $t^0_E$, $t^1_E$ and $t^0_\cup$, $t^1_\cup$, respectively, and $\mu_E$, $\mu_F$, and $\pi_F$ are $*$-homomorphisms, the desired result follows.
\end{proof}
\begin{lemma}\label{reglemma}
Let $E\cup_mF$ be a regular adjunction graph such that 
\[
E^0_{\rm sing}\cap m(G)^0_{\rm reg}\subseteq  p(F)^0_{\rm reg}. 
\]
Then
	$\ker\mu_E\subseteq \pi_E(\ker \pi_F)$, where $\mu_E$, $\pi_E$, and $\pi_F$ are $*$-homomorphisms in the diagram~\eqref{c*diag}.
	\end{lemma}
	\begin{proof}
	By Lemma~\ref{factker}, $\ker\mu_E$ and $\ker \pi_F$ are gauge-invariant ideals with the associated admissible pairs $(m(G)^0,m(G)^0_{\rm sing})$ and $(p(F)^0,p(F)^0_{\rm sing})$, respectively. Since $\pi_E$ is surjective and gauge-equivariant, $\pi_E(\ker\pi_F)$ is a gauge-invariant ideal. Therefore, by the classification of gauge-invariant ideals (see~Theorem~\ref{idealclass}), it suffices to prove that
	\[
	(Y^0_{\pi_E(\ker \pi_F)},Z^0_{\pi_E(\ker \pi_F)})\subseteq (m(G)^0,m(G)^0_{\rm sing}),
	\]
where $Y^0_{\pi_E(\ker \pi_F)}$ and $Z^0_{\pi_E(\ker \pi_F)}$ are given by~\eqref{admideal}. 

First, take a vertex $v\in Y^0_{\pi_E(\ker\pi_F)}$. Since $m(G)^0=Y^0_{\ker\mu}$ by Lemma~\ref{factker}, it suffices to show that $f(v)=0$ for all $f\in C_0(E^0)$ such that $t_E^0(f)\in\ker\mu_E$. To this end, take any $f\in C_0(E^0)$ satisfying $t_E^0(f)\in\ker\mu_E$. By Tietze extension theorem, there is $f'\in C_0((E\cup_mF)^0)$ such that $f'|_{E^0}=f$ and $f'|_{F^0\setminus G^0}=0$.
Let $w\in F^0$ and consider $f'(p^0(w))$. If $w\in F^0\setminus G^0$, then $f'(p^0(w))=f'(w)=0$. Furthermore, if $w\in G^0$, then $f'(p^0(w))=f(m^0(w))=0$ because $t_E^0(f)\in\ker\mu_E$. Therefore, $f'\circ p^0=0$. Since $\pi_F(t_\cup^0(f'))=t_F^0(f'\circ p^0)$, we conclude that $t_\cup^0(f')\in\ker\pi_F$. In turn, we get that \[
t_E^0(f)=t_E^0(f'|_{E^0})=\pi_E(t_\cup^0(f'))\in\pi_E(\ker\pi_F).
\] 
Hence, $f(v)=0$ and we are done.

Next, we prove that 
	\[
	Z_{\pi_E(\ker \pi_F)}\subseteq m(G)^0_{\rm sing}.
	\]
	By contradiction, suppose that there exists $v\in Z_{\pi_E(\ker \pi_F)}$ such that $v\notin m(G)^0_{\rm sing}$. Since
	\[
	Z_{\pi_E(\ker \pi_F)}\subseteq E^0_{\rm sing}\cap Y_{\pi_E(\ker \pi_F)}\subseteq E^0_{\rm sing}\cap m(G)^0,
	\]
	we conclude that 
	$v\in E^0_{\rm sing}\cap m(G)^0_{\rm reg}$,
	which implies that $v\in p(F)^0_{\rm reg}$ by our assumption. Consequently, $v\notin p(F)^0_{\rm sing}$. %, where the last inclusion follows from Proposition~\ref{subp}. 
Since $(E\cup_m F)^0$ is locally compact Hausdorff (and hence Tychonoff), $p(F)^0_{\rm sing}$ is closed, and $v\notin p(F)^0_{\rm sing}$, there exists a function $f\in C_0((E\cup_mF)^0)$ such that $f(w)=0$ for all $w\in p(F)^0_{\rm sing}$ and $f(v)\neq 0$. Since $Z_{\ker \pi_F}=p(F)^0_{\rm sing}$, we have that
	\[
	t^0_\cup(C_0((E\cup_mF)^0\setminus p(F)^0_{\rm sing}))=(\ker \pi_F+\varphi_\cup(\mathcal{K}(X_{E\cup_mF})))\cap t^0_\cup (C_0((E\cup_mF)^0)).
	\]
	Since $f\in C_0((E\cup_mF)^0\setminus p(F)^0_{\rm sing})$, we infer that
	\[
	t^0_\cup(f)\in\ker \pi_F+\varphi_\cup(\mathcal{K}(X_{E\cup_mF})).
	\]
	Now, notice that
	\[
	\pi_E(\varphi_\cup(\mathcal{K}(X_{E\cup_mF})))=\varphi_E((\pi_E^1)^{(1)}(\mathcal{K}(X_{E\cup_mF})))\subseteq\varphi_E(\mathcal{K}(X_E)).
	\]
	Subsequently, we obtain that
	\[
	t_E^0(f|_{E^0})=\pi_E(t^0_\cup(f))\in \pi_E(\ker \pi_F+\varphi_\cup(\mathcal{K}(X_{E\cup_mF})))\subseteq \pi_E(\ker \pi_F)+\varphi_E(\mathcal{K}(X_E)).
	\]
	However, $f(v)\neq 0$, which contradicts the fact that $v\in Z_{\pi_E(\ker\pi_F)}$.
	\end{proof}
	\begin{remark}\label{remarksing}
	Note that $E^0_{\rm sink}\cap m(G)^0_{\rm reg}=\emptyset$ so it suffices to assume that \[
	\overline{E^0_{\rm sink}}\setminus E^0_{\rm sink}\cap E^0_{\rm inf}\cap m(G)^0_{\rm reg}\subseteq p(F)^0_{\rm reg}.\]
	\end{remark}

We are now ready to complete the proof of the main result.
\begin{proof}
(of Theorem~\ref{mainthm}). From~\cite[Proposition~3.1]{pedersen} it follows that the diagram~\eqref{c*diag} is a pullback if and only if
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(P1)] $\ker\pi_E\cap\ker\pi_F=\{0\}$,
\item[(P2)] $\mu_F^{-1}(\mu_E(C^*(E)))=\pi_F(C^*(E\cup_m F))$,
\item[(P3)] $\pi_E(\ker\pi_F)=\ker\mu_E$.
\end{enumerate}
The condition (P1) follows from Lemma~\ref{capker}. The condition (P2) is true due to the commutativity of the diagram~\eqref{c*diag} and Lemma~\ref{preimage}. Finally, the condition (P3) follows from the commutativity of the diagram and the condition~\eqref{maincond} combined with Lemma~\ref{reglemma}. 
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
We will not pursue it here but one might prove that the C*-correspondence of a~regular adjunction graph $E\cup_mF$ is always a pullback of the C*-correspondences of $E$ and $F$ over the C*-correspondence of $G$. Therefore, an alternative way of proving Theorem~\ref{mainthm} would be to use~\cite[Theorem~3.3]{rs-11} stating under which conditions pullbacks of C*-correspondences give rise to pullbacks of their Cuntz--Pimsner algebras. However, in the case of C*-correspondences coming from topological graphs, these conditions are rather restrictive. First, one would have to assume that $m$ is injective. The other necessary assumptions read: $E^0=E^0_{\rm fin}$, $F^0=F^0_{\rm fin}$, $\overline{s_E(E^1)}$ is clopen in $E^0$, and $\overline{s_F(F^1)}$ is clopen in $F^0$. These conditions ensure that the gauge-invariant ideals of $C^*(E)$ and $C^*(F)$ are in one-to-one correspondence with invariant subsets (cf.~\cite[Corollary~8.23]{mt-05}), i.e. the $Z$ component of admissible pairs $(Y,Z)$ is not needed to classify those ideals. Therefore, our approach gives a more general result for Cuntz--Pimsner algebras coming from topological graphs.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The case of directed graphs}

In this section, we compare Theorem~\ref{mainthm} to an analogous result proved by the third author and P.~M.~Hajac in~\cite[Theorem~6.5]{th-24} in the case of directed graphs. The aforementioned paper deals with one-injective pushouts of directed graphs, which are equivalent to adjunction graphs, and the theorem is formulated using breaking vertices. For reader's convenience, we recall this notion here. Let $E$ be a directed graph and let $H$ be a~subset of $E^0$. The set of {\em breaking vertices} with respect to $H$ is defined to be
\[
B_H:=\{v\in E^0\setminus H~:~|s^{-1}_E(v)|=\infty~\text{and}~0<|s_E^{-1}(v)\cap r_E^{-1}(E^0\setminus H)|<\infty\}.
\]
Note that if $F$ is a regular subgraph of $E$, then $B_{E^0\setminus F^0}=E^0_{\rm inf}\cap F^0_{\rm reg}$. Using our notation, \cite[Theorem~6.5]{th-24} reads: Let $E\cup_mF$ be an adjunction graph of (discrete) directed graphs such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[{\rm (1)}] $m^0$ restricted to $B_{F^0\setminus G^0}$ is injective, and
\item[{\rm (2)}] $(m^0)^{-1}(B_{E^0\setminus m(G)^0})\cap B_{F^0\setminus G^0}=\emptyset$. 
\end{enumerate}
Then, the diagram~\eqref{c*diag} is a pullback diagram in the category of $\mathbb{T}$-C*-algebras and $\mathbb{T}$-$*$-homo\-mor\-phisms, i.e. we have a $\mathbb{T}$-equivariant $*$-isomorphism
\[
C^*(E\cup_mF)\cong C^*(E)\oplus_{C^*(G)}C^*(F).
\]

First, note that in this setting, every adjunction graph is regular by Proposition~\ref{adjfinal2} (directed graphs always satisfy the condition $G^0_{\rm sink}\cap (\overline{F^0_{\rm sink}}\setminus F^0_{\rm sink})\cap F^0_{\rm fin}=\emptyset$). The difference between the condition (1) and (2) above and the condition~\eqref{maincond} of Theorem~\ref{mainthm} comes from a different proof strategy. To be more precise, when proving that the diagram~\eqref{c*diag} is a pullback, one can chose one of two sets of conditions given by~\cite[Proposition~3.1]{pedersen}. In the proof of Theorem~\ref{mainthm}, we chose the condtitions (P1), (P2), and (P3), but in the proof of~\cite[Theorem~6.5]{th-24} the second set of conditions was chosen, namely:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(P1')] $\ker\pi_E\cap\ker\pi_F=\{0\}$,
\item[(P2')] $(\mu_E)^{-1}(\mu_F(C^*(F)))=\pi_E(C^*(E\cup_mF))$,
\item[(P3')] $\pi_F(\ker\pi_E)=\ker\mu_F$.
\end{enumerate}
Notice that (P1') is identical to (P1) and that (P2') is always satisfied due to the surjectivity of $\mu_F$ and $\pi_E$. The conditions (1) and (2) above are needed to prove (P3'). Here $\pi_F(\ker\pi_E)$ might not be an ideal, so we cannot simply use Theorem~\ref{idealclass} to prove it. For discrete graphs, it was enough to use the explicit form of gauge-invariant ideals which are not as manageable for topological graphs. In our present approach, (P2) is more difficult to prove than (P2') but still holds by Lemma~\ref{preimage}. Furthermore, we have the following result.

\begin{lemma}\label{disprop}
Let $E\cup_mF$ be a regular adjunciton graph of (discrete) directed graphs. Then the following conditions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[{\rm (1)}] $(m^0)^{-1}(B_{E^0\setminus m(G)^0})\cap B_{F^0\setminus G^0}=\emptyset$,
\item[{\rm (2)}] $E^0_{\rm sing}\cap m(G)^0_{\rm reg}\subseteq p(F)^0_{\rm reg}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For directed graphs, $E^0_{\rm sing}=E^0_{\rm sink}\cup E^0_{\rm inf}$. Since $E^0_{\rm sink}\cap m(G)^0_{\rm reg}=\emptyset$ and $F^0_{\rm sink}\cap G^0_{\rm reg}=\emptyset$ (see, e.g. Remark~\ref{remarksing}), we conclude that $E^0_{\rm sing}\cap m(G)^0_{\rm reg}=B_{E^0\setminus m(G)^0}$ and $F^0_{\rm sing}\cap G^0_{\rm reg}=B_{F^0\setminus G^0}$. 

(1) $\Rightarrow$ (2). Suppose that (1) holds but $V:=E^0_{\rm sing}\cap m(G)^0_{\rm reg}\cap p(F)^0_{\rm sing}\neq\emptyset$. For any $v\in V$, $(m^0)^{-1}(v)\cap G^0_{\rm reg}\neq\emptyset$ by regularity of $m$. Take any $w\in (m^0)^{-1}(v)\cap G^0_{\rm reg}$. Then $m^0(w)=v\in p(F)^0_{\rm sing}$, which implies that $w\in F^0_{\rm sing}$. Therefore,
\[
w\in (m^0)^{-1}(E^0_{\rm sing}\cap m(G)^0_{\rm reg})\cap (F^0_{\rm sing}\cap G^0_{\rm reg}=(m^0)^{-1}(B_{E^0\setminus m(G)^0})\cap B_{F^0\setminus G^0},
\]
contradicting (1).

(2) $\Rightarrow$ (1). Suppose that (2) holds but there exists $w\in (m^0)^{-1}(B_{E^0\setminus m(G)^0})\cap B_{F^0\setminus G^0}$. Then $w\in F^0_{\rm sing}\cap G^0_{\rm reg}$ and $m^0(w)\in E^0_{\rm sing}\cap m(G)^0_{\rm reg}$. We conclude that $w\in F^0_{\rm inf}$ so that $m^0(w)=p^0(w)\in p(F)^0_{\rm inf}\subseteq p(F)^0_{\rm sing}$ contradicting (2).
\end{proof}
We end this section by noticing that Theorem~\ref{mainthm} generalizes and improves~\cite[Theorem~6.5]{th-24}. In particular, combining Theorem~\ref{mainthm} with Lemma~\ref{disprop}, we see that the condition (1) above is not needed.
\begin{corollary}
Let $E\cup_mF$ be an adjunction graph of directed graphs such that
\[
(m^0)^{-1}(B_{E^0\setminus m(G)^0})\cap B_{F^0\setminus G^0}=\emptyset.
\] 
Then, the diagram~\eqref{c*diag} is a pullback diagram in the category of $\mathbb{T}$-C*-algebras and $\mathbb{T}$-$*$-homo\-mor\-phisms, i.e. we have a $\mathbb{T}$-equivariant $*$-isomorphism
\[
C^*(E\cup_mF)\cong C^*(E)\oplus_{C^*(G)}C^*(F).
\]
\end{corollary}

\section{Further examples and applications}

\subsection{Homeomorphism C*-algebras}

A~{\em topological dynamical system} $(X,\sigma)$ consits of a locally compact Hausdorff topological space $X$ and a homeomorphism $\sigma : X \xrightarrow{} X$. The map $\sigma$ defines an automorphism $\alpha$ of $C_0(X)$ given by $\alpha(f)(x) := f(\sigma^{-1}(x))$. The resulting crossed product C*-algebra $C^*(X) \rtimes_{\alpha} \mathbb{Z}$ is called the {\em homoeomorphism C*-algebra}. By Example 2 of \cite{katsura1}, this C*-algebra can be realised as a topological graph algebra with the topological graph given by $E^0 = E^1:= X, \ s := \sigma , \ r :={\rm id}_X $. Note that, since $\sigma$ is a~homeomorphism, the topological graph $E$ is row-finite. In fact, $E^0_{sg} = \emptyset$ and $s(E^1) = E^0 = E^0_{rg}$. First, let use identify regular factor maps between topological graphs coming from topological dynamical systems.

\begin{proposition}
Let $(X,\sigma_X)$ and $(Y,\sigma_Y)$ be topological dynamical systems and let $\phi:X\to Y$ be a continuous map. Then $(\phi,\phi)$ is a regular factor map if and only if
\begin{enumerate}
\item[{\rm (1)}] $\phi$ is proper,
\item[{\rm (2)}] $\phi$ is injective, and
\item[{\rm (3)}] $\phi\circ\sigma_X=\sigma_Y\circ \phi$.
\end{enumerate} 
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Regular factor maps are proper by definition which is equivalent to the condition (1). Next, the condition (F1) is equivalent to the condition (3). Furthermore, (F2) is tantamount to injectivity of $\phi$. Indeed, if $\phi$ is injective, then $(\phi,\phi)$ clearly satisfies the target-bijectivity condition. On the other hand, if $(\phi,\phi)$ is target bijective then, for every $y\in Y$, there is a unique $x\in X$ such that $\phi(x)={\rm id}_Y(y)=y$. Finally, all topological graphs in sight have only regular vertices, so the condition (F3) is automatic.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
Notice that in topological dynamics factor maps are assumed to be surjective (see, e.g.~\cite[p.~38]{dv-14}).
\end{remark}

Now, let $(X,\sigma_X)$ and $(Y,\sigma_Y)$ be two dynamical systems, and $m: G \subseteq Y \xrightarrow[]{} X$ be such that $(m,m)$ is a regular factor map. Let $X \cup Y$ be the corresponding union and let $\sigma_{\cup}$ be a~homeomorphism of $X \cup Y$ defined by $\sigma_X$ on $X$ and $\sigma_Y$ on $Y$, respectively. Since $\sigma_X$ and $\sigma_Y$ agree on $G$, $\sigma_\cup$ is well-defined. We have the following commutative diagram of topological spaces:
\[\begin{tikzcd}
	& {X\cup Y} \\
	{X} && {Y} \\
	& {G}
	\arrow[from=2-1, to=1-2]
	\arrow[from=2-3, to=1-2]
	\arrow[from=3-2, to=2-1]
	\arrow[from=3-2, to=2-3]
\end{tikzcd}.\]
Dually, we obtain a pullback diagram in the category of commutative $\mathbb{Z}$-C*-algebras and $\mathbb{Z}$-$*$-homomorphisms:
\[\begin{tikzcd}
	& {C_0(X\cup Y)} \\
	{C_0(X)} && {C_0(Y)} \\
	& {C_0(G)}
	\arrow[from=1-2, to=2-1]
	\arrow[from=1-2, to=2-3]
	\arrow[from=2-1, to=3-2]
	\arrow[from=2-3, to=3-2]
\end{tikzcd}\]
Clearly, the above union $X\cup Y$ defines a regular union topological graph $(X \cup Y, X \cup Y,\sigma_{\cup}, {\rm id}_{X\cup Y})$.
Therefore, due to Theorem~\ref{mainthm}, we have the following pullback of C*-algebras:
\[\begin{tikzcd}
	& {C_0(X\cup_mY)\rtimes_{\alpha_{\cup}}\mathbb{Z}} \\
	{C_0(X)\rtimes_{\alpha_{X}}\mathbb{Z}} && {C_0(Y)\rtimes_{\alpha_{Y}}\mathbb{Z}} \\
	& {C_0(G)\rtimes_{\alpha_{G}}\mathbb{Z}}
	\arrow[from=1-2, to=2-1]
	\arrow[from=1-2, to=2-3]
	\arrow[from=2-1, to=3-2]
	\arrow[from=2-3, to=3-2]
\end{tikzcd}\]
Note that our result recovers a special case of a well-known theorem connecting pullbacks of C*-algebras and the crossed product construction~\cite[Theorem~6.3]{pedersen}.

