\subsection{Continuous-time spin flip dynamics as a limit of PCA's}\label{contsec}
In \cite{ccr2} we studied the time evolution of Gaussian concentration in the context of spin-flip dynamics.
The main motivation was to detect whether transitions in concentration regime are possible, and whether the stationary measure satisfies the
Gaussian concentration bound whenever it corresponds to a high-temperature dynamics. We recall the definition of
a continuous-time spin-flip dynamics, as well as the conditions we impose on the flip rates.
\subsubsection{Continuous-time spin-flip dynamics}
We consider a continuous-time Markov process on $\Omega=\{-1,1\}^{\Zd}$ where at site $i\in\Zd$, and for configuration $\si\in\Omega$ the spin at $i$ flips at rate $c(i,\si)$. The process is defined via its generator, which on local functions $f:\Omega\to\R$ reads
\be\label{spingen}
L f(\eta)= \sum_{i} c(i, \eta) (f(\eta^i)-f(\eta))
\ee
Our assumptions on the rates are the following.


\subsubsection*{Condition A:}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Locality:
\[
%\label{ligam}
M:=\sup_{i\in\Zd}\sum_{j\in\Zd}\,\sup_{\si\in\Omega} \big(c(i, \si^j)-c(i, \si)\big)<+\infty.
\]
\item Translation invariance: $c(i,\si)= c(0,\tau_{-i}\si)$.
\end{enumerate}
This condition ensures existence of the Markovian dynamics with generator $L$ defined in \eqref{spingen}.
Indeed, as proved in \cite[Chapter 1]{ligg}, under Condition A, the closure of the generator $L$ defined in \ref{spingen}
(in $\caC(\Omega)$ equipped with the supremum norm) generates a unique Feller process.
This process generated by $L$
is denoted $\sipr$, and $\si_i(t)$ denotes the spin at time $t$ at lattice site $i$.
We denote $\E_\si$ expectation in the process $\sipr $ starting from $\si$, and $\pee_\si$ the corresponding path-space measure.
We denote the semigroup
$S(t) f(\si)= \E_\si [f(\si(t))]$, which acts as a Markov semigroup of contractions on $\caC(\Omega)$.
Via duality, $S(t)$ acts on probability measures, and for $\mu$ a probability measure on $\Omega$, we
denote by $\mu S(t)$ the time-evolved measure, determined by the equation
\[
\int f \dd\mu S(t)= \int S(t) f \dd\mu.
\]
We also define, following \cite{ligg},
\be\label{epsi}
\epsi= \inf_\eta (c(0,\eta)+ c(0, \eta^0))
\ee
The well-known $M<\epsi$ criterion \cite{ligg} ensures that in this regime, the spin-flip process is uniquely ergodic, i.e., has
a unique invariant measure and every initial measure converges exponentially fast to it.
%In section \ref{sec:TEGCB} we will consider weakly interacting dynamics and need more stringent conditions:








We are interested in the following question: if $\mu$ satisfies $\gcb{C}$, does
$\mu S(t)$ satisfy $\gcb{C(t)}$, and if so, how does $C(t)$ evolve, and does $C(t)$ have a limit when $t\to\infty$.

In this section, we use the PCA approximation of the spin-flip dynamics to approach this question, thereby generalizing the results of \cite{ccr2}
substantially.
More precisely we show the following results.
\ben
\item For every local spin-flip dynamics which can be approximated by PCA's in the sense of \cite{ms2}, the Gaussian concentration bound is preserved in the course of the evolution.
\item If the spin-flip dynamics is in the $M<\epsi$ regime then also the unique stationary measure satisfies $\gcb{C}$.
\een
A spin-flip dynamics with local flip rates $c(i, \eta)$ is approximated by PCA as in \cite{ms2} section 6, i.e.,
\[
p_i(\eta)=
\begin{cases}
\delta c(i,\eta) + o(\delta) \ \text{if}\ \eta_i=-1\\
1- (\delta c(i,\eta) + o(\delta)) \ \text{if}\ \eta_i=+1
\end{cases}
\]
Let us call $P_\delta$ the transition operator of this approximating PCA.
For the contraction rate of this PCA we have $\kappa (P_\delta)= (1+(M-\epsi)\delta) + o(\delta)$ as is proved in \cite{ms2}, section 7 equation (7.3).
Putting $\delta=1/n$, we have, using Corollary \ref{contcol}
\be\label{kapflip}
\kappa(P_{1/n}^n)\leq 
\kappa(P_{1/n})^n=
\left(1+(M-\epsi)\tfrac1n+ o(1/n)\right)^n
\ee
We can then prove the following.
\bt\label{semintlem}
Let $a=M-\epsilon$. Let 
$\{S(t), t\geq 0\}$ denote the Markov semigroup  associated to the generator \eqref{spingen}, where the flip rates satisfy condition A.
Let $\mu$ be a probability measure on $\Omega$ satisfying $\gcb{C}$. Then
for $t>0$, $\mu S(t)$ satisfies $\gcb{C(t)}$ with
\be\label{cr}
C(t)= c \frac{1-e^{at}}{1-e^{a}} + C e^{at}
\ee
If $a<0$, then there exists a unique stationary measure which satisfies $\gcb{C_\infty}$ with
\be\label{cinfty}
C_\infty= \frac{c}{1-e^{a}}
\ee
\et
\bpr
We start with $t=r\in\N$
We have
\[
S(r)= \lim_{n\to\infty} P_{1/n}^{nr}= \lim_{n\to\infty}(P_{1/n}^n)^r
\]
For the contraction coefficient we have
$\kappa(P_{1/n}) = 1+\tfrac{a}{n}+ o(1/n)$, and therefore, via Corollary
\ref{contcol}, for the contraction coefficient of
$P_{1/n}^n$ we have
\[
 \kappa (P_{1/n}^{n})\leq 
 \left(1+\tfrac{a}{n}+ o(1/n)\right)^n
\]
We can then apply item b) of
Theorem \ref{conservation} to conclude that
$\mu P_n^{nr}$ satisfies $\gcb{C(r)}$ with
\[
 C(r)= c \frac{1-\left(1+\tfrac{a}{n}+ o(1/n)\right)^{rn}}
 {1-\left(1+\tfrac{a}{n}+ o(1/n)\right)^n}
 + C\left(1+\tfrac{a}{n}+ o(1/n)\right)^{rn}
 \]
 Then \eqref{cr} for $t=r$ follows
 by taking the limit $n\to \infty$. If $a<0$, then there is a unique stationary measure, and $\mu S(r)$ converges to it as $r\to\infty$. Therefore \eqref{cinfty} follows by taking the limit $t=r\to\infty$ in \eqref{cr}.
To extend beyond the integer case, let us first assume $t= r/N$ with $r,N\in\N$, $N\geq 1$.
Then the semigroup at time $t$ is equal to the semigroup at time $r$ of the generator $(1/N)L$. By homogeneity of the generator in the rates, corresponds to the process with spin-flip rates
$1/N c(i,\eta)$. For this process, the contraction coefficient of $(P^([N]))_{1/n}^n$ becomes
\[
 \kappa ((P^([N]))_{1/n}^n)\leq 
 \left(1+\tfrac{a}{Nn}+ o(1/n)\right)^n
\]
Therefore, we obtain that $\mu S(r/N)$ satisfies $\gcb{C_N(r)}$ with
\[
C_N(r)= c \frac{1-e^{(a/N)r}}{1-e^{(a/N)}} + C e^{(a/N)r}
\]
Therefore we obtain that for rational $t$, $\mu S(t)$ satisfies $\gcb{C(t)}$ with
\be\label{ct}
C(t)= c \frac{1-e^{at}}{1-e^{a}} + C e^{at}
\ee
The result for general $t$ then follows by right continuity of the semigroup.
\epr







