To add the \jlinl{Sumij} matrix to \typedmatrices{}, we create a new Julia type and implement its core functionality, including dynamic element generation and property checking.
The following code defines the \texttt{Sumij} type.

\begin{jprint}
import Base: getindex, size

struct Sumij{T<:Number} <: AbstractMatrix{T}
    m::Int
    n::Int
end

# Define type constructors.
Sumij(n::Integer) = Sumij(n, n)
Sumij(m::Integer, n::Integer) = Sumij{Int}(m, n)

# Assign known properties to type.
@properties Sumij [:symmetric, :integer, :positive]

# Overload LinearAlgebra functions.
size(A::Sumij) = (A.m, A.n)
LinearAlgebra.isdiag(A::Sumij) = A.n <= 1 ? true : false
LinearAlgebra.ishermitian(::Sumij) = true
LinearAlgebra.isposdef(::Sumij) = false
LinearAlgebra.issymmetric(::Sumij) = true
LinearAlgebra.adjoint(A::Sumij) = A
LinearAlgebra.transpose(A::Sumij) = A

# Dynamic generation of matrix elements.
function getindex(A::Sumij{T}, i::Integer, j::Integer) where {T}
    @boundscheck checkbounds(A, i, j)
    return T(i + j)
end
\end{jprint}




Once defined, the matrix can be used as any Julia matrix.
For example, the following code generates and displays $5 \times 5$ \texttt{Sumij} matrix.

\begin{jinput}[code:mymatrix_example]
Sumij(5)
\end{jinput}
\begin{joutput}
