\subsection{Trade-off between Performance and Memory}

For algorithms that are not implemented in \typedmatrices{}, the package trades performance off for—potentially substantial—memory savings. For example, generating the variable \jlinl{a}, which is of type \jlinl{Cauchy}, only requires \textbf{63.229 $\boldsymbol{\mu}$s} and \textbf{114.16 KiB} of memory, while generating \jlinl{b}, which is the same matrix but has type \jlinl{Matrix}, requires \textbf{3.862 ms} and \textbf{7.74 MiB} of memory. And once generated, storing \jlinl{b} requires 500,000 times more memory than storing \jlinl{a}.

\begin{jinput}
@benchmark a = Cauchy{Float64}(1000)
@benchmark b = Matrix(Cauchy{Float64}(1000))
@show Base.summarysize(a)
@show Base.summarysize(b)
\end{jinput}
