In summary, we make the following contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item We propose a novel prompt design paradigm that challenges conventional wisdom in which \textit{unnatural language prompts} pruned from random ICL demos could outperform prior state-of-the-art prompt optimization approaches, which we verified through large-scale experiments. The relatively consistent improvements for \textit{random} ICL prompts suggest a potential effective approach for ICL stabilization;  
   % \item Empirically, we validate the effectiveness of such design paradigm through large-scale experiments on a collection of ten LLMs across eleven NLP tasks. Notably, we show that such paradigm applies to LLMs regardless of alignment, suggesting a broad misspecification of unnatural language for current LLMs;
    \item Conceptually, motivated by the unnatural language observations, we propose a novel conceptual framework that reformulates prompt compression as guided prompt search seeking out improvements in both task performance and inference speed;
    \item Practically, we propose \textsc{PromptQuine}, a novel prompt optimization framework that evolves effective pruning strategies in a manner mimicking open-ended biological evolution. This framework showcases state-of-the-art performance across diverse tasks, while significantly improving search runtime compared to existing token-level search algorithms;
    \item  Mechanistically, we investigate the role of label words, as what have done for conventional ICL \cite{min-etal-2022-rethinking, wei2023larger}. We show that most of the conclusions generalize in our context of ICL pruning, with one exception that pruning could be helpful with random verbalizers. Future research is needed to advance the understandings of such unnatural ICL behaviors. 
\end{enumerate}

