%\paragraph{Jailbreaking Additional Details.} Here, we elaborate on the construction of our fitness function using steering vectors. Steering vectors are a concept derived from recent research on activation steering \cite{kim2018interpretability, zou2023representation}, which enables direct manipulation of LLM inference behavior within its internal activation space. This typically assumes that each atomic prediction feature (e.g., for particular task output) is associated with a single global direction in activation space, and that adding this direction can directly influence the prediction towards improving the performance of a particular task of interest. A variety of existing studies \cite{todd2023function, liucontext, rimsky-etal-2024-steering} have demonstrated the effectiveness of this approach across a wide range of tasks. We provide a detailed explanation of how the fitness function is derived using steering vectors below: We mostly follow \cite{ball2024understanding}'s approach for the jailbreaking steering vector construction. Specifically, we derive the \textit{mean difference} vector from the contrastive prompt pairs--queries formatted to contain the same request, with and without jailbreak attempts embedded in their templates. We take one of the templates from \cite{ball2024understanding}. We provide one such example in Table . We select layer 14 according to its effectiveness observed in our preliminary experiments. This echoes previous work suggesting that intermediate layers tend to be most effective for contrastive activation steering. We then calculate the residual-stream activation differences at the last instruction token between the jailbreak and non-jailbreak prompts in our separated 100 validation samples. This difference vector serves as the jailbreaking concept vector. We measure its representational similarity by cosine similarity at the same precise model location and use this information to guide pruning. Ideally, higher similarity reflects stronger jailbreaking signals. Finally, we apply our \textsc{PromptQuine} framework to perform the guided search.

