We perform an ablation study by removing some key components from our GA designs. Our observations reveal that, in the absence of these components, populations tend to evolve until they plateau at suboptimal local optima. In other words, simplified GA designs often result in populations becoming ``trapped'' in inferior regions of the search space. We present the analysis below, especially discussing around the offspring size, the population size, as well as the choice of mutation rates. We perform experiments based on standard generational GAs. We present studies with respect to \textit{regularized evolution} in the Appendix \ref{app:stagnation-ablation}. We show that \textit{regularized evolution} outperforms various diversity-preserving mechanisms in addressing the premature convergence we frequently observe.

