\section{Introduction}

% EAs~\cite{EibenBook2003} have proven to be adept mechanisms for 


% Network topology plays a key role in 

% ~\cite} reference work that will look at

%A novel update to the design of DNN's architectures came about in the last decade with the introduction of multiple branching and skip connections~\cite{lecun2015deeplearning}, as seen with groundbreaking architectures like googLeNet~\cite{szegedy2015going} and ResNet~\cite{he2016deep}.
% A novel advancement in the design of deep neural network (DNN) architectures emerged in the last decade with the introduction of multiple branching and skip connections~\cite{lecun2015deeplearning}. This innovation was exemplified by groundbreaking architectures such as GoogLeNet~\cite{szegedy2015going} and ResNet~\cite{he2016deep}. Multiple-branch architectures can extract feature information in a parallelised fashion, where each branch can focus on aspects of the input, such as patterns of different resolutions, textures and edges. This allows the architecture to encompass a more expansive range of feature information, improve their generalisation ability and help stabilise gradients~\cite{xu2024development}. As such, multi-branch mechanisms are incorporated into many modern state-of-the-art architectures and have been widely adopted in many different problem domains; such as image recognition~\cite{szegedy2015going}, autonomous vehicles~\cite{teichmann2018multinet} and medical diagnostics~\cite{kumar2018breast,das2018multiple}.


%Hand-crafting the design of DNN architectures, however, is a challenging prospect, which often require expert knowledge or trial-and-error approaches to finding well-performing architectures. To this end, considerable attention has been given to Evolutionary Algorithms (EAs)~\cite{EibenBook2003}, which can be used in the automatic configuration of DNNs, either by evolving their network topologies, hyperparameters and/or weights, a process known as neuroevolution~\cite{Galvn2021NeuroevolutionID}.
% Hand-crafting the design of DNN architectures, however, is a challenging prospect that often requires expert knowledge or trial-and-error approaches to identify well-performing architectures. To address this, considerable attention has been given to Evolutionary Algorithms (EAs)~\cite{EibenBook2003}, which can be used for the automatic configuration of DNNs by evolving their network topologies, hyperparameters, and/or weights---a process known as neuroevolution~\cite{Galvn2021NeuroevolutionID}.

%. The aim of this evolutionary process, referred to as neuroevolution~\cite{Galvn2021NeuroevolutionID}, is to find high-quality network architectures based on their performance which are typically attained by some measure of fitness relative to the problem domain at hand.  

%% discuss the relative increase in search space by scalling topologies

%There are two central challenges to performing neuroevolution effectively. First, is the significant high computational overhead required in finding high-quality solutions and second, is the arbitrarily large search spaces involved in searching for all potential architectures.

One of the primary challenges to performing neuroevolution effectively is the significantly high computational overhead required in finding high-quality solutions. It has been noted, that for large DNN models, the cost of training a single epoch can be prohibitive, where this issue is further compounded when many networks are required to train. One way to tackle this expense is through the use of surrogate models, where a distance metric can be used to input the performance of a network without the need to fully train it. One of the major challenges of this approach is comparing networks with different topologies. While distance metrics can be created to compare such topologies using genotypic information, i.e., information pertaining to the structure of the architecture itself, these methods require clever encoding strategies for variable-length architectures. Moving to graph-based topologies further complicates the issue as the genotypic representations would require graph-edit metrics to compare distances~\cite{sanfeliu1983distance}, which can be computationally inefficient to calculate for larger graphs~\cite{ gaier2018data}.    
%\sout{This novel approach is an update of the chain-based (single branch) representation first proposed by Stapleton et al.~\cite{stapleton2024ola}~\cite{Stap2406:NeuroLGP}}.

To this end, we propose Neuro-Linear Genetic Programming Multi-branch (NeuroLGP-MB) which uses Linear Genetic Programming (LGP)~\cite{brameier2007linear} to cleverly encode the diverging and converging paths of a multi-branch topology.  Furthermore, this approach makes use of a semantic-inspired approach to creating distance metrics for surrogate modelling. Semantics, which refer to the output of a program when executed on a given set of inputs, have been widely studied and adopted within the genetic programming (GP) community~\cite{DBLP:conf/gecco/GalvanS19,galvan2022semantics}. However, their study and application in neuroevolution remain underexplored, with only a few exceptions~\cite{Stap2406:NeuroLGP,stapleton2024ola}. The most important aspect of using semantics within this context is that the fixed lengths of these semantic vectors are invariant to architectures of varying length and of different topological complexity, allowing for imputation for a vast array of different topologies, such as those found in multi-branch architectures. 

%\sout{The core aspects of this paper and main contributions, have been summarised below:}

Thus, the main goal of this work is to find well-performing DNNs by evolving their architectures, which involve multi-branch connections, while doing so efficiently by employing surrogate-assisted EAs. The main contributions of this work are:
i) the use of an ingenious representation based on LGP that allows us to evolve multi-branch connections in DNNs, referred to as NeuroLGP-MB, ii) the use of a semantic-based approach that allows us to make a leap from using dozens/hundreds to thousands of sample points for our Surrogate-assisted EAs model, aligning with current and complex DNNs, iii) the use of a more informative Surrogate-assisted EA model that samples high-quality individuals, which are used to initialise our surrogate model. This method significantly enhances the evolutionary search, while simultaneously reducing the search process significantly, all while still attaining high performance compared to the other three approaches used in this work: the simple Surrogate-assisted EA model, baseline, and expensive approaches, iv) and an analysis on the elite members of the population, looking at how depth and complexity changed over time, demonstrating the topological preferences for the NeuroLGP-MB method on the tested datasets.
% \item an analysis on varying the initial epoch number used to inform the surrogate model, gaining insight into the potential time saving (up to $\sim$42\%), as well as offering a discussion on other aspects of varying epochs.


