% Next, we perform an analysis of the topological complexity of the network. While the number of parameters is often used to denote network complexity, in this analysis, we focus primarily on topological complexity. To this end, we define complexity based on the layers that result in nodes (i.e., layers) with a degree greater than 2 (i.e., nodes with more than 2 edges). This approach highlights the implications of allowing the evolution of multi-branch connections in DNNs. As such the concatenation layer, is the sole contributing layer to complexity, we focus our analysis on the proportion of concatenation layers relative to the other layers in each architecture and while this does not give us an exact measure of the width of the neural network, it does give us some indication of the overall topological complexity. 



As the concatenation layer was the contributing factor in determining network topological complexity, we performed an analysis of their proportions relative to the elite population members in the final generation specifically. We found that typically the number of concatenation layers ranged from 1 in 33 layers to 1 in 8 layers, depending on the dataset and run. This showed a preference for relatively narrow networks during search. In general, this analysis would indicate that during evolution there was a particular focus on depth, with a tendency to find deeper networks which are relatively less topologically complex. Despite this, we found that some experiments were preferencing concatenation layers for certain runs. Future work could look at promoting concatenation layers and other branching operations through mutation and crossover.

