\begin{abstract}

% Revised by Moontae
Efficient access to high-quality information is vital for online platforms. To promote more useful information, users not only create new content but also evaluate existing content, often through helpfulness voting. Although aggregated votes help service providers rank their user content, these votes are often biased by disparate accessibility per position and the cascaded influence of prior votes. For a fairer assessment of information quality, we propose the \modelFullName (\modelName), a causal framework that accounts for the context in which individual votes are cast. Through preliminary and semi-synthetic experiments, we show that \modelName effectively models the position and herding biases, accurately recovering the predefined content quality. In a real experiment, we demonstrate that reranking content based on the learned quality by \modelName exhibits stronger alignment with both user sentiment and quality evaluation assessed by GPT-4o, outperforming system rankings based on aggregated votes and model-based rerankings without causal inference. Beyond the individual quality inference, our embeddings offer comparative insights into the behavioral dynamics of expert user groups across 120 major StackExchange communities.

%Beyond the individual quality inference, our community embeddings offer comparative insights across 382 StackExchange communities, contrasting behavioral dynamics of expert user groups.

%Efficient access to valuable information is essential for online platforms. To promote more useful information, users do not only produce new content, but also evaluate existing content. Casting helpfulness votes is a prevalent notion for assessing the quality of information, empowering service providers to rank their content discriminately. Although helpfulness votes are informative in aggregate, voting is rarely unbiased in interactive systems. The ordering of information causes disparate accessibility. Existing votes on individual information influence future votes. To mitigate position and herding biases for a more fair assessment, we must answer counterfactual questions such as 'What if the information was presented at different ranks' or 'What if the information had received even votes?' Modeling such a doubly reinforcing generative process between position and herding biases, we propose \modelName, a novel algorithm which is not only able to infer fair quality of information, but also to predict the probability of the future vote by forcing counterfactual settings. Our real data experiment demonstrates that reranking the content based on estimated quality shows better alignment with both user comments and GPT-4o evaluation than the original helpfulness vote ranking and the reranking of existing Chinese Voting Process (CVP) model \citep{lee2016beyond}. We also include a semi-synthetic data generator based on the proposed model using real data coefficients and size. The semi-synthetic experiment verifies the advantages of \modelName. In addition, community embeddings provide users with comparative insights across different communities, potentially helping service providers to better design their interactive interfaces.
\end{abstract}

